    {
      "id": "source-3",
      "type": "review-note",
      "reviewer": "OpenAI Codex",
      "reviewedAt": "2026-08-24T19:10:16Z",
      "note": "Reviewed the live Ethereum receipts and call traces for the anchored free-share mint and follow-up withdrawal, the RewardsHypervisor creation trace, pre-exploit runtime and code hash, verified source, the commit-pinned reproduction, Beosin's analysis, and Rekt's incident account. The attacker contract calls deposit(100,000,000e18, attackerContract, attacker) and, when RewardsHypervisor calls its owner() and delegatedTransferERC20() hooks, returns itself as owner, transfers no VISR, and reenters deposit once. RewardsHypervisor then calls vVISR.mint twice for 97,624,975.481815716136709737 vVISR each. The follow-up withdrawal burns the resulting 195,249,950.963631432273419474 vVISR and transfers exactly 8,812,958.138426966035592617 VISR to the attacker. RewardsHypervisor is a full direct runtime with no DELEGATECALL or SELFDESTRUCT; its creation and pre-exploit hashes match and the code remained unchanged. Published incident accounts value the withdrawn VISR at approximately $8.2 million."
    },
